course: dictionary
lesson: 15698
title: Toleration and letting others be different
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Toleration means letting other people hold beliefs, follow customs or behave in ways that you may not agree with, and not trying to stop them. «We show toleration when we let our friend support a different football team.» You may not agree, but you let them choose.
explain: In history, toleration can mean that a government lets people follow different religions freely. «Religious toleration means people of every faith can pray in their own way.» The key idea is allowing, not forcing.
explain: Toleration is not the same as agreeing. You can disagree with someone and still show toleration. Look for clues in the words around it: if people are being left alone to live their own way, toleration is probably the right meaning.
fact: Toleration is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say «a spirit of toleration», not «a toleration».
fact: The verb is tolerate, so people can tolerate things they do not like.
fact: «Toleration» means allowing beliefs, customs or behaviour you may not share, without trying to stop them.
fact: «Religious toleration» means a government allows people of different faiths to follow their religions freely.
fact: «Tolerance» means much the same as toleration, and it can also mean being able to put up with something unpleasant.
exercise: «Our school shows toleration when everyone can wear their own religious clothing.» What does toleration mean here? | Shouting at people who look different | Forcing people to agree with each other | Making everyone wear the same uniform | *Letting people be different without trying to stop them
exercise: Which is the best meaning of «religious toleration»? | A quiet room for praying | *A government allowing people of different faiths to follow their religions freely | Stopping people from praying | Only one religion is allowed in the country
exercise: «Ben's toleration of the noise from the building site was impressive.» What does toleration mean here? | Making the noise louder | Being very happy about the noise | *Being able to put up with something unpleasant | Wanting to stop the noise
test: «Our class has a spirit of toleration, so everyone can share their ideas.» What is a spirit of toleration? | Being quiet in class | Telling others what to think | *Being open to other people's ideas and ways, even when they differ from yours | Being very fast at games
test: Which sentence shows toleration? | *Sam let his friend choose the film, even though Sam wanted a different one. | Sam laughed at people who look different. | Sam hid his friend's bag. | Sam told his friend to be quiet.
exam: Which best describes toleration? | *Allowing others to hold beliefs and customs you may not share, without trying to stop them | Agreeing with everything everyone says | Refusing to let others have their own beliefs | Making rules that only one group can follow
